coronavirus perhaps the biggest crisis  our country has faced for a generation  what do you make of the way the  government has handled things so far and  just before answering that and on the  back of Rosanna's interview I just think  we should note the enormous debt of  gratitude we owe to our NHS frontline  staff have been absolutely extraordinary  to everyone working in the NHS and to  the care is paid and unpaid who are  bearing the brunt of this crisis so I  just wanted to to say I think the entire  nation is looking at them and saying  this is the best of what we have to  offer as a country and we should be very  grateful to them and look this is an  unprecedented challenge and there's I  don't want to be in a position of  offering criticism of the government I  think they'll be working as hard as they  possibly can they're doing everything  they can they will be mobilised in the  entire resources of government I think  and I offered this not as a criticism  but as constructive advice in my view it  is all about getting to mass testing as  fast as possible because we have to know  who has the disease and who has had the  disease in order to get the lockdown  eased and get people back to work and  get some semblance of normality back  into our daily life so I think the only  thing I would say is for me this is  right from the very beginning it's been  about of course organizing the NHS to  respond to this crisis but the issue of  testing both the PCR tests to say who  has the disease and the antibody tests  to say who has had it and who therefore  may be immune to it these are the  absolutely crucial things I think and  it's interesting isn't you say that  could be one route out of the lockdown  because obviously people been talking  about a potential vaccine but do you  think that if this mass testing is  rolled out not just to NHS staff but to  ordinary people as well that could be  the more realistic way of easing the  measures well I think it's the only way  of easing the measures ultimately  because look obviously the NHS frontline  staff come first in in testing but what  what is the conundrum of the coronavirus  crisis the conundrum the dilemma if you  like is that only a small number of  people are actually in the end going  have a life-threatening version of it  but a small percentage of a very large  number is still a large number and  that's why it's absolutely right that  we're taking the measures of social  distancing and the lockdown  but in the end the vast majority of the  population will be capable of going back  to work and will either be have had the  disease and ramune or have had a milder  version of the disease or won't get it  at all but the problem is unless you  know who's had it or who has it and  those that don't then it's very hard to  see how you you you return to normal  life because your risk obviously is as  you start to ease the lockdown how do  you then deal with any resurgence of the  disease and this of course is what  they're now dealing with in in China and  South Korea and elsewhere but unless you  have that testing capability that you  can apply it's scale and by the way when  I say mass testing I mean I actually  think you will need to get to the point  where you've got the capability and I  assume you're preparing for this now of  testing literally a very large  proportion of the entire population and  you may have to do those tests two or  three different times because you need  all the time to be able to track what's  happening with the disease to learn  where for example there may be a surge  or a hot spot of it and take immediate  action and what kind of percentage of  the population are you talking about  well I think you'll be talking about  virtually everybody I mean this the  problem with the the corona virus is  that it's obviously highly contagious  and although it's true that some people  will suffer no symptoms at all I mean if  you look at the studies coming out of  