ctly possible  to do that that depends how how illy is  but if I and again I offer this not as  criticism because I really think  everybody in government will be doing  everything they possibly can but I think  what you find in these situations I went  through a much much milder thing with  foot-and-mouth disease and my Institute  also work throughout the a bola crisis  in in West Africa it is always around  logistics and procurement what you find  in these situations is that you get  bottlenecks not about what you think you  should do because you can usually work  that out but how you do it how you  source equipment how you get supplies  how you make systems move fast and the  only thing I would say on that and it's  really the Prime Minister's perfectly  able to direct this I provided his  health is has not deteriorated but it's  really about how you put the most  capable people in charge of those  of logistics and procurement and I think  the other thing we should be doing right  now by the way is putting everything we  can behind technology and innovation  there will be a thousand things that  technology can help us with particularly  when we need to get the data as to who's  had it and who hasn't had it how for  example when we start to get back to  work and you're trying to trace people  when you may get an outbreak how you  managed to trace all the people they've  had contact with I think there's a  there's a mobilisation of the private  sector alongside the public set here  that is important and might and as I say  I offer this just from my own experience  dealing with these things you sometimes  find that the best people on this are  not to be found in the public sector but  in the private sector for example people  who do procurement logistics that's what  they do for a living every single day I  think mobilizing all that effort is also  going to be important and one other  thing we shouldn't forget this is a  global crisis and by the way if any  country in this crisis and some of the  poorest countries of the world where  we're trying to help at the moment and  whose health care systems will be  completely overwhelmed by this disease  if any country in the world is not over  this disease then all countries are at  risk and this is the classic situation  in which a global pandemic means if you  don't have global coordination and  global solutions then everyone is  affected I want to talk a little bit  about that point that you make and it is  of course the global crisis and every  country potentially could be affected  here how concerned are you about what  coronavirus would mean to countries for  example in Africa right so my  Institute's got partnerships with  roughly 15 16 different governments in  Africa including of the largest  countries there and you know I'm seeing  firsthand from the conversations I'm  having with presidents and with others  in the World Health Organization you  know we've got a huge challenge I mean  an unprecedented challenge but these are  healthcare systems that are already  quite weak and government systems that  aren't all was as effect  they need to be they've got a massive  shortage of equipment ventilators  protective clothing masks testing of  course and in the end if these countries  and they're just at the beginning of the  curve now if these countries aren't  helped at this stage then it's going to  slow down the ability of the world as a  whole to get over this crisis and I  still think in a round global  coordination I don't quite understand  why why there isn't there aren't streams  of work going on where scientists are  coming together to make sure that we  develop the vaccine as swiftly as  possible and industries are being  brought together across the globe so  that when this vaccine is is found and  manufactured you're able to distribute  vast amount of its and distribute it  fairly  and why for example we're not we know  that that ventilators and respiratory  equipment is going to be extremely  important we know this may last for a  year you know what is the global  mechanisms pulling all this together and  accelerating it so it's it's very  difficult for those countries in Africa  and elsewhere then at some of the  poorest countries in the world I mean  they are going to find this very very  tough and it's worth I know our priority  will be everyone here at home of course  it will be but you know we need to  realize that in the end if there's a one  part of the world that manages to get on  top of this in another part of the world  that can't then in the end we're all  going to have to deal with it you talk  about the need for a global response to  coronavirus but at the same time what  we've also seen of course is just how  quickly it can spread across the world  do you think it also shows some of the  risks of globalization to globalization  people that people want to travel that  the migration is just a fact of modern  life and technology joins us up all the  time and only Europe you're not going to  stop that process unless you stop people  traveling to different countries and all  the trade and commerce that actually has  made the the world more prosperous but  what we do need to do and the warning  signs have been there for a long time I  mean after a bola by the way it was very  clear  and Botha was limited fortunately to a  small part of West Africa and we were  able to get on top of it and deal with  it but there were those at the time who  said you know take this as a warning of  what will happen if you get a global  pandemic when you know you get a disease  that spreads very easily and the problem  with covert 19 is that it does spread  very easily so you know out of this for  sure as as well as the mechanisms of  economic coordination to make sure that  we reflate and re-energize the world  economy we should be taking steps that  allow us to have the global framework  and networks in place that if this  happens again and it will happen again  then we're better prepared to deal with  it ok Tony Blair thank you very much for  being on the program as women I think

